\hypertarget{orai1-panels}{%
\section{Orai1 Panels}\label{orai1-panels}}

The Orai1 chapter runs two ligand panels through the same three pipelines against the same four receptor frames, and the two panels were not sampled at the same settings. The experimental panel, which pairs the Orai1 modulators with the four frames, inherited the sampling budget of the calibration benchmark, namely thirty AutoDock Vina modes inside a 6 kcal/mol energy window, thirty DiffDock samples and thirty unguided EquiBind conformers. The control panel pairs all 308 calibration ligands with the same four frames, giving 1,232 complexes, and was cut back to ten Vina modes inside a 3 kcal/mol window, ten DiffDock samples and ten unguided EquiBind conformers. Both settings are given side by side in Table~\ref{tab:appendix-orai-protocols}. The two panels share the four receptor frames, the whole-receptor search boxes, the exhaustiveness of 32, the AutoDock seed and the post-docking variant carried forward for each tool. They are therefore matched on search settings rather than on every input, and the three further differences that remain are stated in the paragraphs below, namely the receptor converter, the DiffDock sampler seed and the in-protein UFF relaxation on the EquiBind arm.

The Orai1 yield, transmembrane-placement and pose-clustering analyses are then taken over the top ten ranked poses of each frame-ligand unit, and that cap removes most of the asymmetry rather than concealing it. For AutoDock Vina the two panels searched on the same settings, because the box, the exhaustiveness and the seed are the same and the mode count and the energy window govern only how many of the resulting modes are written out. The receptor files themselves were not prepared by the same converter, since the control receptors were written by MGLTools and the experimental receptors that enter the analysis by Meeko, so the two panels are matched on search settings rather than on every input file. The first ten modes of a thirty-mode run are therefore the same ranked prefix that a ten-mode run reports, and the twenty deeper experimental modes are discarded before analysis. The narrower control window was not binding either, because all 1,232 control complexes wrote the full ten modes, so no complex was truncated by the 3 kcal/mol limit before the cap applied. The DiffDock seed is a further recorded difference, since the reproducibility hook that fixes the sampler seed appears in the experimental run logs and is absent from the control ones.

For DiffDock and EquiBind the prefix argument is weaker, so the residual difference is stated rather than argued away. Both tools rank a pool of independently generated samples, so the ten retained experimental poses are the best ten of thirty whereas the retained control poses are simply all that were written. For EquiBind that is ten of ten on every unit. For DiffDock it is ten of ten on 1,037 of its 1,215 control units and fewer than ten on the remaining 178, ten of which return a single pose, so its control denominator varies by unit. The experimental panel therefore enters every comparison with the more heavily selected pose set. That bias runs against the direction of the reported result, because the control panel returns the higher usable yield for all three tools, so a deeper experimental pool would if anything have narrowed the gap rather than opened it. One further difference is confined to EquiBind, namely that the experimental poses passed through the in-protein UFF relaxation while the control poses did not, the relaxation having been switched off after the calibration runs showed it distorting ligand geometry to relieve clash. The EquiBind arm of the two-panel comparison is therefore not a clean per-panel match and is read as such.

